<h2 id="launch"><span class="sec-mark" aria-hidden="true">§ II</span> Launch commands</h2>

<p class="sec-lead">Each entry's command, exactly as registered. The Claude Code client is what starts these servers, and they run on your own machine with the project directory as their working directory (nothing is dialled out to a hosted endpoint).</p>

<div class="plate">
  <div class="plate-bar">
    <span>.mcp.json</span>
  </div>
  <pre class="plate-body">{% for m in roster.mcpDetail %}<span class="t-ok">{{ m.name }}</span>
  {{ m.command }}
{% endfor %}</pre>
</div>

<p>The three <code class="tok">npx</code> entries pull from npm the first time they launch, whereas <code class="tok">sprint-channel</code> runs from the overlay itself. That is why org mode needs no extra install, and also why every session in a pod must share a single checkout.</p>

<h2 id="merge"><span class="sec-mark" aria-hidden="true">§ III</span> Merge behaviour</h2>

<p>If you already have a <code class="tok">.mcp.json</code>, install and upgrade will merge into it rather than replace it. Servers the template names belong to the baseline and refresh from it, which is how you pick up new baseline arguments and environment settings. Servers the template does not name are yours, and those stay byte for byte as they were.</p>

<p>To keep a change of your own, put it under a name the template does not use. Change one of the template's own servers and the next upgrade will overwrite it, because for those names the template wins.</p>

<p>Top-level keys outside <code class="tok">mcpServers</code> behave the same way: the merge adds template keys you are missing and leaves the keys you already have. Where the result would match your file exactly, nothing is written at all and the upgrade reports the file unchanged.</p>

<h2 id="replacing"><span class="sec-mark" aria-hidden="true">§ IV</span> Replacement</h2>

<p>Article VI.5 requires that a third-party API be checked against current documentation before any code is written against it. The rule names the outcome and leaves the tool open, so while <code class="tok">context7</code> is the shipped default you may swap it out or drop it entirely, provided the checking still happens. A library's own <code class="tok">llms.txt</code> will do, and so will a pinned local copy of the docs.</p>

<p><code class="tok">audit-baseline</code> checks these servers by name and not by count. If you swap one out, the audit will tell you which name it expected to find.</p>
